# Rust
target/
# Cargo.lock is intentionally committed for reproducible firmware builds.
# Libraries omit Cargo.lock; binaries/firmware commit it.
# See: https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/faq.html
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb

# IDE
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Build artifacts
*.bin
*.elf
*.hex
*.map
*.o
*.d

# Simulator output
simulator_output/

# Hardware tools
*.kicad_prl

# Temporary datasheet/reference screenshots (except committed golden files)
*.png
# Allow golden screenshot reference files for visual regression tests
!**/tests/golden/*.png
*.kicad_pcb-bak
_autosave-*
fp-info-cache
*.bak
*.net

# Atopile
#
# NOTE: .gitignore has NO inline comments. These two patterns used to read
#   `hardware/.ato/          # Atopile package cache`
# which git treats as a single pattern INCLUDING the spaces and the '#', so it
# matched nothing and neither directory was ignored. It went unnoticed because
# the toolchain was broken for months — no build ran, so nothing was ever
# generated to be accidentally committed. The first successful build staged
# `hardware/build/logs/latest`.
#
# Atopile package cache (like node_modules)
hardware/.ato/
# Generated build output: logs, BOMs, manifests, manufacturing data
hardware/build/
# Per-module `check-*` layouts.
#
# atopile creates `<layout>/<build-name>/<build-name>.kicad_pcb` for every build
# target, so the eight per-module validation builds each produce one. They are
# never routed and carry no design intent — only `default/` is the real board,
# and that one IS committed because `--frozen` diffs against it.
hardware/elec/layouts/check-*/

# Manufacturing
/hardware/manufacturing/gerber/*.zip

# 3D CAD
*.step.lock
*.FCStd1

# Logs
*.log

# Temporary files
tmp/
temp/

# Scratch / codegen files (double-underscore prefix convention)
__*

# Throwaway test output files
test_*.txt

# Git worktrees
.worktrees/

# Credentials — distributor API keys. NEVER committed.
# `.env.example` is the committed template; the negation must stay last so the
# template survives the wildcard above it.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Sourcing catalogue — the wide discovery surface. Rebuildable from the vendor
# APIs (a 400-part category is ~20 calls), and deliberately NOT committed: CI
# reads hardware/bom/snapshot.json, so a part dropping to zero stock arrives as
# a reviewable diff rather than silently changing under us.
hardware/bom/catalogue.db
hardware/bom/catalogue.db-journal
hardware/bom/catalogue.db-wal
